# `\xNN` is a CHAR-literal escape only (tests/char_hex_escape.ty). A string
# literal still refuses it, and the reason is the adjacent-literal join, not the
# hex: joining is defined on the literals' ESCAPED source text and is sound only
# while every escape is exactly two characters (docs/spec/01-lexical.md §3.9.4),
# so a greedy C-style `\x` could absorb a hex digit across a join.
#
# This fixture is the guard on that: it must stay red for as long as string
# literals reach codegen as escaped text (src/tychoc.c:467-473).
fn main():
    println("\x41")
